# Noma Enterprise workspace

::summary
Noma's enterprise workspace is a modular monolith for **Docs**, **Visuals**, and **Work**, with shared identity, authorization, assets, versioning, relationships, search, notifications, changesets, and audit. This page records what the in-repo kernel implements and what remains a deployment or partner-validation concern.
::

## Implemented in this tree

The `EnterpriseWorkspace` kernel (`src/enterprise.ts` and `src/enterprise-*.ts`) is the R0–R3 executable slice: identity, visual Docs, PaperDOM, native Work depth, changesets, search/knowledge evals, fixture-backed Confluence/Jira adapters, HTTP/worker processes, and the section-17 demonstration. Live IdP, AWS/EU hosting, and paid-pilot usability remain external constraints with in-repo contracts and tests.

| Area | What is implemented | Evidence |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Identity | OIDC adapter interface, SCIM upsert/deprovision, short-lived sessions, revocation | `test/enterprise-workspace.test.ts` |
| Docs | Persistent block/table IDs, lossless adapter, draft/publish coordinator, CRDT persist-before-ack, reconnect replay, lost-ack idempotency, independent concurrent edits | `test/stable-identity.test.ts`, `test/enterprise-crdt.test.ts` |
| Visuals | PaperDOM kernel with server-derived actors, revision preconditions, outlines/alt text, asset scan/quarantine | PaperDOM and asset tests |
| Work | Configurable types/workflows, hierarchy, boards, sprints, worklogs, custom fields, JQL subset with unsupported-field reporting, bulk-edit preview, issue security, throughput/cycle-time/CFD reports, automation dry-run | `test/enterprise-work-depth.test.ts` |
| Agents | Changeset lifecycle, no self-approval, untrusted payload policy, kill switch, outbox, six recipes that emit changesets, 40-task scripted agent benchmark | changeset tests, `test/enterprise-http.test.ts` |
| Knowledge | Permission-aware search and relations; citations carry resource/block/hash; RAG eval harness; stale/contradiction queues | `test/enterprise-knowledge.test.ts` |
| Migration | Inventory dispositions; Confluence storage XML and Jira JSON adapters; SSRF denylist; cutover state machine | `test/enterprise-connectors.test.ts` |
| Operations | SQLite enterprise store, PostgreSQL DDL generator, digest-checked backup/restore, legal hold, tamper-evident audit chain, health/readiness, SBOM, redacted support bundles | backup, hold, and ops tests |
| HTTP / worker | `apps/server`, `apps/worker`, `apps/workspace` entry points over the same kernel | `test/enterprise-http.test.ts` |
| Packages | Extraction boundaries under `packages/{document-core,document-ui,platform,work,knowledge,connectors,contracts,paperdom-core}` re-export the CLI kernel (no dual-write copies) | package `src/index.ts` files |
| E2E | Section 17 demonstration: two humans, one agent, connector, pin, stale apply, revocation, restore | `test/enterprise-e2e.test.ts` |

## Not claimed as production-ready

The following remain **outside** this kernel's passing tests and must not be advertised as shipped enterprise capabilities. Each has an in-repo contract or fixture so the gap is explicit rather than silent:

- Hosted Tiptap/Yjs/Hocuspocus replication across real browsers (the kernel persists an operation log and refuses overlapping live heads; it is not a Yjs network)
- Pinned PaperDOM packages extracted from the standalone PaperDOM repository (in-tree `paperdom-core` is the host contract)
- Live Confluence/Jira Cloud or Data Center HTTP adapters (storage/JSON fixtures and cutover stages only)
- Bedrock/EU reference deployment, KMS, malware-scanning products, independent security assessment, and assigned incident ownership
- Blinded usability studies and three paid pilots with named champions
- Comparative LLM-vs-Markdown agent scoring with a partner corpus (the 40-task harness is scripted)

SQLite remains the technical-preview datastore. PostgreSQL is the documented enterprise target; `enterpriseSchema("postgres")` emits JSONB/BYTEA DDL for that migration, and application backup/restore is tested independently of RDS snapshots.

## Package map

Canonical logic lives in `src/enterprise-*.ts` so the published CLI stays a flat `dist/*.js` pack. `apps/server|worker|workspace` are process entry points. Workspace packages re-export `@ferax564/noma-cli` and must not become unpublished `workspace:` dependencies of the CLI.
